            <ArticleTitle>The pro-oxidant activity of high-dose vitamin E supplements in vivo.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>271-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A recent meta-analysis has demonstrated that the regular administration of high-dose vitamin E supplements may be associated with increased mortality. The biological mechanism for this effect is uncertain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A ferrous oxidation xylenol assay was used to assess plasma oxidation activity levels in samples from a randomized, placebo-controlled, 6-week trial of daily vitamin E supplementation in adults with asthma (n = 72).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A 27% increase in plasma oxidation activity levels was observed in patients receiving vitamin E. We demonstrate a pro-oxidant effect of high-dose vitamin E supplementation that may explain the increase in mortality observed in intervention studies using this nutrient.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
